<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alpujarras</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Las Alpujarras</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alpujarras is a mountainous region on the southern slopes of the Sierra <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nevada">Nevada</a> in the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Under the Nasrids it was a densely settled land of terraced, irrigated farms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> production. It was the centre of two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Morisco</a> revolts, in 1499–1501 and 1568–1571, after which its Morisco population was deported and replaced by settlers from northern Spain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alpujarras.jpg" alt="Alpujarras" width="320" height="293" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Xufanc · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Alpujarra.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Alpujarra lies on the south slopes of the Sierra Nevada and the valley below, at an average height of 1,200 metres. Its name most probably comes from the Arabic al-basharāt, roughly sierra of pastures. The range runs about 80 km from west to east and includes the Mulhacén, at 3,479 metres the highest mountain of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Most of the snow melts in spring and summer, so the southern slopes stay green, fed by innumerable springs channelled to terraces and villages.</p><p>Olives grow on the lower slopes and in the valley of the Guadalfeo river from Órgiva to Cadiar, with grapes, citrus and almonds nearby. The eastern end, towards Ugijar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a>, is much drier. The administrative centres are Órgiva in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and Alhama de Almería.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Spanish Muslim people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, who lived here for centuries after the conquest of 711, built the terraces and irrigation of the upper Alpujarra and villages of narrow winding streets and small flat-roofed houses in the manner of the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. The revolt of 1568 was a civil war fought with much cruelty on both sides, ending with the death of the last Muslim leader in March 1571. The entire Muslim population of the territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> was then taken on forced marches to other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>; only a few judged true converts stayed to teach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> industry.</p><p>Settlers came from all over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, many from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, but resettlement went badly: a population estimated at 40,000 before the last revolt was about 7,000 by the end of the century. The region stayed poor and isolated until roads opened it in the 20th century. Nationalist and Republican forces fought over it in the Civil War of 1936 to 1939, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> held out in the mountains until their leader was captured in 1942.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The high villages have lost people to the cities, while tourism and foreign residents have brought income. Serrano ham cured in Trevélez and other high villages is a major product, the GR 7 / E4 footpath crosses the region, and the Sierra Nevada and most of the upper Alpujarra are protected.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alpujarras” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Intensive irrigated farming in the Vega of Granada and the <b>Alpujarras</b> produced cereals, fruit, olives and sugar cane, while mulberry trees supported a silk industry whose cloths were prized across the Mediterranean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-granada">Emirate of Granada · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It began the Rebellion of the <b>Alpujarras</b> against mass forced conversions.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-18">18 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
